},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", ..."},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" were on the root after "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th move. Then the other tiles that we put inside the tree at the beginning have no effect in the final result. Then we can suppose that only these tiles were initially put on tree."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"So we can assume that all tiles we place at the beginning will reach to the root together. Suppose "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" of these tiles were put at a vertex with depth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the maximum depth that there is at least a tile in that depth. So as to these tiles reach to the root together we must pay "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ecad84215573826a4681665820da48dd31dce644c85cc633384c7f5773c3f97f.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then we want to minimize the number of needed coins so at the beginning there must not be two consecutive depth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and there is a tile at depth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and an empty vertex at depth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1. In other words if we denote the minimum depth that initially there is a tile inside it as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" then there must be a tile at each vertex with depth more than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and less than or equal to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's iterate over "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then for each "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" we can calculate "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", the minimum depth that we can pay the needed price if we put a tile at each vertex with depth at least "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and at most "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let's denote this needed price as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then we can also put "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/021661ee1d985cd8e1f23a01813cc8c5d5d88159351c6175f1d70731f3ebf00f.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" at depth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1. So we can calculate maximum number of tiles that we can put on the tree so that they all reach to root together for a fixed "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". So the maximum of these numbers for all possible "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" will be the answer."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Since by increasing "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" won't decrease one can use two-pointers to update "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" while iterating over "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let's denote number of the vertices with depth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cnt"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then we can save and update the following values."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f9b545da40c260d4491a7d15948a747ce46c8142452a4e99b543f31b2f563af8.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/36abea673142afbb88e0d54c6e37ed735493788fc1ba29d4ecd4ad82110a80ca.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then the needed price is equal to ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". So as long as ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we must increase "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". This yields an "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") solution."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/414/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"414E - Mashmokh's Designed Problem"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's define the dfs-order of a tree as the sequence created by calling function dfs(root). We'll build another sequence from a dfs-order by replacing each vertex in dfs-order by '+1' and inserting a '-1' after the last vertex of its subtree. Note that all vertices of a particular subtree are a continuous part of dfs-order of that tree. Also note that for each vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" if the +1 corresponding to it is the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th element of sequence, then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s distance from root(which we'll denote by height of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") is equal to sum of elements 1.."},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Suppose we can perform the following operations on such sequence:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For each "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", find sum of the elements 1.."},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For each "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" find the biggest "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") so that sum of elements 1.."},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of the sequence equals "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Using these two operations we can find LCA of two vertices "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", so since distance of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" equals to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"height"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"height"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") - 2 * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"height"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"LCA"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")) we can answer the second query. Also the third query can be answered using the second operation described above."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"As for the first query it cuts a continuous part of sequence and insert it in another place. This operation can be done using implicit treap. Also we can use the treap as a segment tree to store the following values for each vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then using these values the operations described above can be done. All of these operation can be done in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"logn"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Sum of the elements in its subtree(each vertex in the treap has a value equal to +1 or -1 since it corresponds to an element of the sequence.)"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's write the values of each vertex in the subtree of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the order they appear in the sequence. Then lets denote sum of the first "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" numbers we wrote as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ps"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] and call elements of ps, prefix sums of the subtree of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then we store the maximum number amongst the prefix sums."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Also we'll store the minimum number amongst prefix sums."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/11470"}